\description{
Coordinates of each sample in an orthonormal basis of the Aitchison
simplex: Egozcue et al.'s isometric log ratio (ilr). Euclidean distances
between them are Aitchison distances, for any basis.
}
\details{
For a sample \eqn{x} with clr coordinates \eqn{\mathrm{clr}(x) = \log(x +
c) - \overline{\log(x + c)}} (natural logs, \eqn{c} the pseudocount) and a
basis \eqn{\Psi} with orthonormal rows,
\deqn{\mathrm{ilr}(x) = \mathrm{clr}(x)\,\Psi^T.}
The default basis is applied by a running sum, in \eqn{O(D)} per sample,
and other bases through their non-zero entries, so an SBP costs only its
non-zeros.

Because the coordinates are orthonormal, \code{euclidean(ilr(x))} is
\code{aitchison(x)} (with the same pseudocount), whichever basis is used: \strong{for
distances, use \code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}}}. \code{ilr()} is for analyses of the coordinates
themselves: regression, \code{\link[=lda]{lda()}} or \code{\link[=rda]{rda()}} without the clr's singular
covariance, and balances that can be interpreted, from an SBP. The coordinates feed straight into \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}},
\code{\link[=rda]{rda()}}, \code{\link[=lda]{lda()}}, \code{\link[=envfit]{envfit()}} and \code{\link[=euclidean]{euclidean()}} with \code{norm = 'none'}.

\code{ilr(x + c)} here equals scikit-bio's \code{ilr(x + c)} (and
\code{ilr(x + c, sbp_basis(sbp))}) to rounding.

\strong{Same names in other packages}: \code{compositions::ilr()} and
\code{robCompositions::pivotCoord()} compute ilr coordinates with other default
bases (\code{compositions::ilr()}'s default is this one with the opposite
sign). Whichever package was attached
last masks the other's \code{ilr()}; write \code{ecodive::ilr()} to be sure.
}
\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

Logs of zero are undefined, so zeros need replacing first. The
pseudocount is added to every value (not only to zeros), as in
\code{normalize(norm = 'clr')}. With \code{pseudocount = NULL} (the default), no
pseudocount is used when \code{counts} has no zeros; otherwise half the
smallest non-zero value is, with a warning.

The choice of pseudocount is not neutral: it can distort the results,
especially for sparse tables. See \code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}} for references.
}

\examples{
    x <- ilr(ex_counts, pseudocount = 1)
    dim(x)

    # Euclidean distances on ilr coordinates are Aitchison distances
    all.equal(
      as.vector(euclidean(x, norm = 'none')),
      as.vector(aitchison(ex_counts, pseudocount = 1)) )

    # Balances from a sequential binary partition of 4 features
    sbp <- rbind(
      c(1,  1, -1, -1),
      c(1, -1,  0,  0),
      c(0,  0,  1, -1) )
    ilr(ex_counts[, 1:4], basis = sbp, pseudocount = 1)[1:3, ]

}
